{
  "_comment": "The committed settings file ({settings_file}) every adopted repo carries (enforcement/README.md section 4.4, decision 3). Three keys. `hooks`: one entry point, claude-hook.py, run with the hook's id; it reads the event JSON on stdin and exits 2 with the reason on stderr to refuse. Schema, matchers and the exit-code contract are the ones Anthropic documents at https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks (quoted in claude-hook.py's docstring). Every path in this file is a placeholder naming a key of the layout table (layout.json beside the checks, task E5.1), filled by adopt.py when it writes the project's settings: it installs claude-hook.py at {session_hook}, which is the path these commands name, and the checks it runs at {checks_dir}. Each statusMessage names the hook's id, which is how verify_rules.py resolves a rule's session:<id> reference. `permissions.deny`: the hard denies, in the syntax of https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions — Anthropic says to use the permission system, not a hook, for a hard deny; Claude Code applies a deny rule inside subshells, $(...) and loops and past VAR=value, whatever a hook says. The rules name the mutating forms (deploy, destroy, delete, create, apply, ...) and not the programs, because a deny rule cannot carry exceptions and the read-only forms (fly status, aws s3 ls, terraform plan) must stay open; the hook explains a refusal in words. The Edit rules hold the governing paths every platform shares. `disableAllHooks: false`: a project false overrides a user true, so a user setting cannot switch these hooks off (only `--settings '{\"disableAllHooks\": true}'` on the command line can). adopt.py writes all three: hooks and disableAllHooks replaced from here, the deny list merged in front of the project's own; every other key is the founder's and kept.",
